  </div>

  <p>
    If you are noticing that your assumptions about "government is safer" or "private is better
    infrastructure" do not hold up once you check the actual data for your field, that is useful
    information now, not something to discover after you have already committed four or five years to a
    specific college.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not decide this on the category label alone, and do not decide it on a campus tour or a
    persuasive admission pitch either.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Place your field on the government-weight spectrum honestly, get the branch-wise placement data
      for the specific colleges you are considering in writing, check recognition status directly on
      the official portal, and plan a real skill-and-proof-of-work layer alongside whichever degree you
      choose. That combination decides your actual career outcome far more reliably than the word
      "government" or "private" printed on the certificate.
    </p>
    <p>
      A strong skill portfolio and visible proof of work, built alongside any college you attend,
      moves you toward stronger income and earlier financial freedom faster than the category label on
      the degree ever will by itself.
    </p>
  </div>

